window.docContents[18].resolve({"/Dependency-Graph/#Strong-Multiplicity-One-___-Lean-blueprint--Dependency-Graph":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Strong Multiplicity One — Lean blueprint","header":"Dependency Graph","id":"/Dependency-Graph/#Strong-Multiplicity-One-___-Lean-blueprint--Dependency-Graph"},"/The-old___new-decomposition/#Strong-Multiplicity-One-___-Lean-blueprint--The-old___new-decomposition":{"contents":"At level , the cusp forms split into the old subspace \n() — the span of the degeneracy images of forms of proper\ndivisor level — and the new subspace \n(), its orthogonal complement under the Petersson inner\nproduct (). The old part  is the projection\nof a cusp form onto ; a newform is a normalised eigenform\nlying in .\n\nIf a cusp form shares all (away-from-) Hecke eigenvalues with a newform, then its\nold part vanishes (); dually, a newform never lies in the\nextended old subspace. Hence a form whose eigenvalue system equals that of a newform\nis forced into the new subspace.\n\nThe old subspace is built from level-raises of lower-level forms, whose eigenvalue\nsystems are exactly those occurring at proper divisor levels. A newform of level \nhas, by definition, an eigenvalue system that is genuinely of level  — it does\nnot occur at any proper divisor (this is ).\nSo if a form's eigenvalues match a level- newform's, none of the old-subspace\ngenerators can contribute, and its old part is zero.\n\n","context":"Strong Multiplicity One — Lean blueprint","header":"2. The old/new decomposition","id":"/The-old___new-decomposition/#Strong-Multiplicity-One-___-Lean-blueprint--The-old___new-decomposition"}});